  "worked_solution": "First, we note that the function is a positive cubic curve because the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is positive.\n\nWe are told that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so to find the other roots of $f(x)$ we use synthetic division to divide $f(x)$ by  $(x-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & -9 & 18 \\\\  &  & 2 & 0 & -18 \\\\  & 1 & 0 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-2)({x}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =(x-2)(x-3)(x+3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the roots of $f(x)$ are $x=-3,$ $x=2,$ and $x=3.$ \n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-2)(0-3)(0+3) \\\\  & =(-2)(-3)(3) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-3,2,3$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=18,$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234519/assets/066d0669762137e2e014c2b76c941bdf7812c2d537ae1c03e0ae89a1d7107dc3.png)",
